WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.240
SpaceX plans 10,000 annual starship launches from Louisiana alone, more than every provider on Earth combined. This is SpaceX Daily Episode 144, falcon 9 from vandenberg, a starlink evacuation in Colombia, and a methane rocket for orbital data centers follow. Then the counterpoint, the ai front, and the engineering angle. Elon Musk said on X that the planned Gulf Coast facility alone will support that cadence. That works out to about 27 flights a day.

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:35.000
every day of the year. Louisiana is one node in a larger network, with more pads coming

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:40.780
at Starbase, Texas, and other sites. The operation would need rapid turnaround, high vehicle reuse,

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:46.280
and factory-scale ground support instead of traditional one-off spacecraft campaigns. His

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:52.360
wording applies to Louisiana only, which implies still more capacity from other sites. He posted

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:55.640
the target while discussing raw capabilities in a separate thread.

00:00:55.640 --> 00:01:01.486
Vandenberg Space Force Base sent up a Falcon 9 carrying 27 Starlink satellites.

00:01:01.866 --> 00:01:06.606
It was the 72nd launch that California Pad has supported in 2026.

00:01:07.246 --> 00:01:12.446
California has now passed Florida in total orbital attempts this year, tying or exceeding

00:01:12.446 --> 00:01:16.906
Florida's annual count for the first time in recent years, because Starlink missions

00:01:16.906 --> 00:01:17.926
have shifted west.

00:01:18.466 --> 00:01:21.646
The booster completed its flight profile and recovery sequence.

00:01:22.226 --> 00:01:24.986
SpaceX confirmed the payload deployed the same morning.

00:01:24.986 --> 00:01:28.726
Vandenberg logged the flight as part of its record-setting pace.

00:01:29.621 --> 00:01:34.861
A Starlink terminal provided the only reliable link for a medical evacuation in rural Columbia.

00:01:35.521 --> 00:01:40.041
Local responders used it to coordinate transport for a mother and newborn in real-time.

00:01:40.541 --> 00:01:45.881
The remote site has no terrestrial infrastructure, and no other connectivity options were available.

00:01:46.641 --> 00:01:50.441
Elon Musk highlighted the case as the network-reaching isolated communities.

00:01:50.961 --> 00:01:54.581
The incident fell on the same day he posted the Louisiana launch target.

00:01:54.581 --> 00:02:00.424
it. Cowboy Space outlined a two-stage methane rocket for orbital data centers, according

00:02:00.424 --> 00:02:06.924
to Space.com. The vehicle stands 86 meters tall and uses 9 Clydesdale engines on a reusable

00:02:06.924 --> 00:02:12.524
first stage. Its upper stage is designed to stay in orbit as a one-megawatt AI-compute

00:02:12.524 --> 00:02:17.504
satellite, becoming the data center spacecraft itself so there is no separate payload bus.

00:02:18.084 --> 00:02:23.824
The company plans a first flight in 2028 and a constellation of 20,000 units called Stampede.

00:02:23.824 --> 00:02:29.964
Each satellite will carry five racks of NVIDIA Vera Rubin accelerators and deploy radiators

00:02:29.964 --> 00:02:32.084
plus solar panels after reaching orbit.

00:02:32.664 --> 00:02:37.364
The first stage is planned to land on a ship at sea, and Cowboy Space filed for Federal

00:02:37.364 --> 00:02:42.224
Communications Commission or FCC Authorization covering the Stampede Network.

00:02:43.255 --> 00:02:48.475
Elsewhere, SciGuy Space noted red flags in the Cowboy Space Business case while confirming

00:02:48.475 --> 00:02:53.355
the company is offering competitive compensation to experience propulsion and avionics engineers.

00:02:53.355 --> 00:02:59.555
The Grock-Bott Interface, Elon Musk posted, functions like a capable on-demand staff member

00:02:59.555 --> 00:03:01.315
for routine and technical tasks.

00:03:02.272 --> 00:03:07.732
NASA Space Flight reported parallel processing flows for booster 23, and the vehicles assigned

00:03:07.732 --> 00:03:10.032
to flights 15 and 16 at Starbase.

00:03:10.692 --> 00:03:15.532
Observers treated the activity as evidence of increasing production parallelism ahead of

00:03:15.532 --> 00:03:17.092
the next two integrated tests.

00:03:17.492 --> 00:03:22.752
Those flows let several boosters and ships move through integration and testing, so one vehicle

00:03:22.752 --> 00:03:28.694
is not blocking the next. Scott Kirby, chief executive of United Airlines, called Starlink

00:03:28.694 --> 00:03:33.334
the biggest edge United holds in the United States market right now, according to Tesla

00:03:33.334 --> 00:03:33.734
Roddy.

00:03:34.254 --> 00:03:38.714
He said that edge will shrink once other airlines field similar systems, and he called

00:03:38.714 --> 00:03:44.297
the window temporary. Worth keeping an eye on is a non-engineer observer who flag multiple

00:03:44.297 --> 00:03:49.097
classic warning signs in cowboy space's pitch for an orbital data center constellation.

00:03:49.097 --> 00:03:54.877
The comments reflect ongoing community caution toward new entrance promising rapid timelines

00:03:54.877 --> 00:03:56.557
against established operators.

00:03:57.077 --> 00:04:00.757
The pitch includes an unproven upper stage to satellite transformation.

00:04:01.743 --> 00:04:07.123
On the AI front, cursor added an annotate tool in design mode, letting people draw on the

00:04:07.123 --> 00:04:11.823
page to steer the model across several screen sizes, according to a post on X.

00:04:12.323 --> 00:04:16.443
The feature expands the ways developers can guide model edits without writing code.

00:04:16.443 --> 00:04:22.003
Useers can now mark elements, sketch changes, and test layouts across device sizes within

00:04:22.003 --> 00:04:22.843
the same session.

00:04:23.654 --> 00:04:28.574
For the engineering angle on that golf coast cadence, every ground operation has to become

00:04:28.574 --> 00:04:31.914
a repeatable industrial process rather than a one-off campaign.

00:04:32.454 --> 00:04:37.194
At that rate, the pad and tower have to take a returning vehicle, load propellant, and

00:04:37.194 --> 00:04:42.314
lift off in about 50 minutes on average, with almost no spare time for inspection or repair.

00:04:42.314 --> 00:04:49.174
A day holds 1,440 minutes, so a 50-minute cycle is the entire clock if the tower never

00:04:49.174 --> 00:04:53.774
sits idle. The mechanical arms that caught the super heavy booster on earlier flights

00:04:53.774 --> 00:04:59.314
already show one piece of that speed. Extending the same approach to the ship upper stage removes

00:04:59.314 --> 00:05:04.674
the need for separate landing legs and recovery ships. Legs are dead mass you fly on every

00:05:04.674 --> 00:05:10.214
ascent. A tower that catches the ship absorbs that mass on the ground, which cuts both structure

00:05:10.214 --> 00:05:12.614
and the hour spent inspecting landing hardware.

00:05:13.094 --> 00:05:18.114
Each system you delete lowers mass and turnaround time, and that is how you attack the idiot

00:05:18.114 --> 00:05:18.574
index.

00:05:19.054 --> 00:05:22.134
How many times the finished stack costs over its raw inputs?

00:05:22.694 --> 00:05:28.274
The raw material floor is stainless steel sheet, liquid oxygen and methane, plus the silicon

00:05:28.274 --> 00:05:31.134
and rare earth content inside avionics and engines.

00:05:31.714 --> 00:05:36.674
The magic wand number, metal plus propellant, plus those minerals, is a few hundred thousand

00:05:36.674 --> 00:05:37.574
dollars per flight.

00:05:37.574 --> 00:05:42.974
The finished vehicle still carries an idiot index of roughly 50 to 100 times that floor

00:05:42.974 --> 00:05:46.714
once labor, tooling, testing, and certification are included.

00:05:47.274 --> 00:05:51.734
A few hundred thousand dollars times 50 is on the order of tens of millions for a flight

00:05:51.734 --> 00:05:56.674
ready stack before reuse, as a back of envelope figure, not a company price.

00:05:57.294 --> 00:06:02.274
Fly the same stack many times and that labor and certification burden spreads across flights,

00:06:02.654 --> 00:06:05.294
which is the only way the ratio comes down at scale.

00:06:05.294 --> 00:06:10.254
rail. Rapid reuse and high launch cadence are the levers. There is no materials trick

00:06:10.254 --> 00:06:15.014
left that moves the floor as much. Commercial aviation and container shipping made the

00:06:15.014 --> 00:06:21.054
same shift from custom jobs to scheduled, high utilization cycles, and cost fell. A narrow

00:06:21.054 --> 00:06:26.354
body airliner turnaround is on the order of 30 to 45 minutes, and that rhythm is what made

00:06:26.354 --> 00:06:31.694
seats cheap. Standardized containers did the same for ports. The crane in the box became

00:06:31.694 --> 00:06:36.934
the product, not a bespoke ship for every cargo. Starship's stainless steel construction

00:06:36.934 --> 00:06:43.034
and methalox, methane plus liquid oxygen, already sit below kerosene or hypergolic systems

00:06:43.034 --> 00:06:49.234
on the raw material floor. Cryogenic fill is a heat transfer problem. Liquid oxygen flashed

00:06:49.234 --> 00:06:54.334
into a warm tank boils off, so the ground system has to keep the lines cold between flights.

00:06:54.934 --> 00:06:59.654
At airline-like frequency, chill down, pump time, and crew movement become the product.

00:06:59.654 --> 00:07:05.134
not the welding of the next hull. If the mechanical tower arms and the Gulf Coast site deliver

00:07:05.134 --> 00:07:10.394
even 20% of the announced cadence, per-flight marginal cost falls far enough to support

00:07:10.394 --> 00:07:15.614
orbital data center and refueling missions. If even a fraction of the stated rate arrives,

00:07:16.094 --> 00:07:20.834
the bottleneck shifts from building vehicles to moving cryogenics and ground crews without pause.

00:07:21.494 --> 00:07:27.723
By the close, shares finished a little over 1% higher. Before we go, those star-based parallel

00:07:27.723 --> 00:07:31.643
L flows are feeding the next two integrated tests on flights 15 and 16.

